1	// Acceptance test for the change request:
2	//   "Make sure that discount shows up everywhere I check what is owed. That means the invoice, the
3	//    statement and the home screen."
4	//
5	// An invoice can already carry a percentage DISCOUNT, and its detail page shows the subtotal, the
6	// discount and the final total (see invoice-discount.spec). What this change adds is that the
7	// discount must now flow through into "WHAT IS OWED" — the money still to be paid — in the three
8	// places the app answers that question:
9	//
10	//   1. THE INVOICE — how much is still left to pay on an invoice (invoice-due-amount, shown on each
11	//      invoice's row in its project's invoice list). Before this change it was the invoice's GROSS
12	//      amount minus payments; after it, it is the DISCOUNTED total (amount minus the discount) minus
13	//      payments.
14	//   2. THE STATEMENT — each invoice row's due (statement-invoice-due) and the client's outstanding
15	//      total (client-outstanding-total) on the client statement. These net off the discount as well
16	//      as payments.
17	//   3. THE HOME SCREEN — the dashboard's money owed (dashboard-outstanding-total), which sums the
18	//      DISCOUNTED totals of the sent invoices, not their gross amounts.
19	//
20	// WHY THESE VALUES. The discount in money is subtotal × percentage, and the discounted total is the
21	// subtotal (the invoice's amount) minus that. "What is owed" is then that discounted total minus any
22	// payments. The figures below are chosen so that only a figure that has genuinely had the discount
23	// taken off can be right — a value that still showed the gross amount (the pre-change behaviour), or
24	// that took the discount off only some of the invoices, is wrong in at least one assertion:
25	//   - a $500 invoice at 10% is a $450 total; with $100 paid, $350 is still owed (not $400, which is
26	//     the gross $500 less the $100 paid; not $450, which forgets the payment; not $500, the gross);
27	//   - an $800 invoice at 25% is a $600 total; unpaid, $600 is owed (not the gross $800);
